            <div class="legal-section">
                <h2 id="open-source-and-this-agreement">1. Open Source and This Agreement</h2>
                <p>CertiFried&rsquo;s source code, including the bot, the dashboard, the stream bot and CertiFried Studio, is published at <a href="https://github.com/xXDeath420Xx/LiveBot" rel="noopener">github.com/xXDeath420Xx/LiveBot</a> under the <a href="https://github.com/xXDeath420Xx/LiveBot/blob/dev/LICENSE" rel="noopener">GNU AGPL-3.0-or-later</a>. Two documents, two jobs:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>The AGPL governs the code.</strong> It is the binding licence for copying, running, studying, changing and sharing CertiFried&rsquo;s code, and it is the only licence you need to do those things. If you run a changed version as a service for other people, the AGPL requires you to offer them its source code. This EULA cannot add restrictions to what the AGPL allows, and if anything here seems to, the AGPL wins for the code.</li>
                    <li><strong>This EULA governs our service and our builds.</strong> It sets the rules for using the service we operate and the CertiFried Studio builds we publish on certifriedmultitool.com: what you may do with our servers, what we promise and what we do not.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>As the AGPL asks of a service like ours, the complete source of what we run is available to everyone who uses it, from the Source code link in the site footer and on the <a href="/legal">Legal and data</a> page. Some parts of the code come from other projects under their own licences, noted in the repository. The author&rsquo;s <a href="https://github.com/xXDeath420Xx/LiveBot/blob/dev/DBAD.md" rel="noopener">Don&rsquo;t Be a Dick note</a> is a friendly request about how to use the code; it is not an extra legal condition.</p>
            </div>

